      "marks": 6,
      "answer": "$a=1$ or $a=\\frac{49}{23}$.",
      "official_mark_scheme_text": "Use the correct scalar-product process for the direction vectors — M1: $(a,2,-1)\\cdot(2,-1,1)=2a-2-1=2a-3$.\n\nUsing the correct modulus process, divide the scalar product by the product of the moduli and equate the result to $\\pm1/6$ — *M1.\n\nState a correct equation in $a$ in any form — A1, e.g. $(2a-3)/(\\sqrt{a^2+5}\\sqrt6)=\\pm1/6$.\n\nSolve for $a$ — DM1. Solve a three-term quadratic after expanding $(2a-3)^2$: $36(2a-3)^2=6(a^2+5)$, $138a^2-432a+294=0$, $23a^2-72a+49=0$, $(23a-49)(a-1)=0$.\n\nObtain $a=1$ — A1.\n\nObtain $a=49/23$ — A1. Allow $a=2.13$.\n\nAlternative cosine-rule method: use $\\cos\\theta=[|\\mathbf u|^2+|\\mathbf v|^2-|\\mathbf u-\\mathbf v|^2]/(2|\\mathbf u||\\mathbf v|)$ with correct vectors — M1; equate the result to $\\pm1/6$ — *M1 A1, allowing M1* for any two vectors; solve the same three-term quadratic — DM1; obtain $a=1$ — A1 and $a=49/23$ — A1.\n\nTotal [6].",
